strict mode 
strict mode is used to identify mistakes in code and makes it easier to debug
just use ---> use 'use strict'; at the beginning of the code if any code is present before it won't work .(commends are ignored by js).


What is a function ?
function is a piece of code we can use over and over .

to use a function (calling,running or invoking) the function is carried out --> functionName()

 return is something the function gives out as a result of it's execution.
we can use this result in other parts of the code and for that we need to save this result in a variable .

Motto of function --> Don't repeat Your code or make dry codes.

parameteres and arguments -parameters are just like variables that you can pnly use inside a function and arguments are actual values inside those variables or the values passed to the functions while calling it ..

function declaration vs function expression

function expressions produce a value and can be stored inside a variable or the function can be used as an expression .
they can't be called before declaration.(no hoisting)
 
In js a statement -->performs an action/ Instruction
expression -->produces a value 

Types of functions

1.Normal /Named function
A function declared using the function keyword and an identifier (name) is called a normal or named
function. The same identifier with parentheses is used to invoke it.
• The arguments object can be used here.
• Function hoisting is allowed for normal/named function declarations.
function demo() {
console.log("I am demo function");
}
demo();
function sum(a, b) {
console.log(arguments);
return a + b;
}
console.log(sum(10, 20));
2.Anonymous function
A function declared without an identifier (function name) is called an anonymous function. It is commonly
stored in a variable so it can be called.
var variableName = function (params) {
// code to execute
};
variableName(args);
var add = function (a, b) {
return a + b;
};
console.log(add(10, 20));
3.Arrow function
An arrow function is a shorter syntax for writing functions in JavaScript. It was introduced in ES6 (2015)
and is commonly useful for callbacks.
const functionName = (parameters) => {
// function body
};
=> is called the fat arrow.
Arrow Function Examples
// No arguments
let arrow = () => {
console.log("arrow function");
};
arrow();
// Multiple arguments
const add = (a, b) => {
return a + b;
};
console.log(add(20, 30));
7. Arrow Function Characteristics
1. No arguments
() => {
// function body
}
2. One argument
let square = a => {
console.log(a * a);
};
square(2);
3. More than one argument
const add = (a, b) => {
console.log(a + b);
};
add(20, 30);
4. Implicit Return
If the function body contains only one expression, the expression can be returned automatically without
using the return keyword.
const square = x => x * x;
console.log(square(3)); // 9
5. Explicit Return
When you use the return keyword explicitly, or when the function contains multiple statements, use curly
braces.
const add = (a, b) => {
return a + b;
};
console.log(add(50, 60));
const greet = (name) => {
console.log("Hello");
console.log(name);
};
greet("John");

